=== REASONING ===

This slice ports the slice-0015 (N1 nl-alert-builder) template wholesale and
re-targets it at the Automation graph DTO surface. The logic the slice 0015
critique already validated still applies, so the same shape gives us the
fastest, lowest-risk path to a green gate while preserving the AI-OFF contract.

Why we re-use the N1 template verbatim:

  1. ADR-015 §I3 (baseline intact) requires the manual AutomationBuilderPage
     graph editor and the canonical decodeAutomationInputDTO validator to
     remain the unconditional baseline. The template is the proven shape that
     already satisfies §I3 for AlertStudio: same withAiFeature HOC pattern, same
     "PROPOSE-only / save flows through the existing typed handler" contract.

  2. ADR-015 §I6 (off-mode 404) requires guard.Wrap to short-circuit BEFORE
     the AI handler runs. The template proves the pattern works (the slice
     0015 Go off-mode test exists and passes); we re-use it to maintain
     evidence consistency across slices.

  3. ADR-015 §I9 (no leakage) requires the redaction decorator to wrap any
     provider call. PolicyAutomationBuilder() is already defined in
     internal/ai/redact/policies.go (deny-all, ModeRedactedTags) — it was
     authored by F8 specifically for this slice, so no new policy is needed.

  4. Slice 0015 critique findings already steered the AI-handler shape:
       - vehicle scope clamp at the tool boundary (not just in the prompt),
       - PROPOSE-only deny-all confirm hook in the dispatcher,
       - kind-discriminated wire-payload builder (no stray null fields hit
         the canonical DisallowUnknownFields decoder),
       - per-tool status='invalid' surface for validator failures (not an
         Execute error) so the LLM's follow-up prose can describe the issue.

Key per-slice deviations from the alert-builder template:

  a) The validator interface accepts json.RawMessage (wire JSON bytes), not a
     typed *models.AlertRule. Reason: decodeAutomationInputDTO performs
     decoding AND validation in one pass and consumes io.Reader, so the AI
     tool feeds it the wire-shape JSON rather than a typed struct. The
     production wrapper AIAutomationGraphValidator uses
     bytes.NewReader(wireJSON) to bridge.

  b) The wire-payload builder maps each per-step kind discriminator to ONLY
     the fields that kind owns. Helpers setNonEmpty/setPtr (generic) prune
     empty/nil values so the canonical decoder's DisallowUnknownFields rule
     never sees a stray null from the LLM's flattened input shape.

  c) Tool registration order: RegisterAutomationBuilderTools is called AFTER
     RegisterAlertBuilderTools in router.go to keep the registry's
     alphabetical Names list deterministic (BuiltinNames pin test depends on
     it).

  d) Goldens use the F6 stub-tool wrapper (any
     {"type":"object","additionalProperties":true} schema) so the canned
     arguments do not need to match the real tool input schema byte-for-byte
     — but they DO parse as valid JSON and exercise:
       - basic_signal_action       (happy path: trigger_signal + action_command)
       - refusal_other_vehicle     (cross-vehicle scope refusal — strategy I9)
       - refusal_disable_existing  (modify-existing refusal — propose-only)


